ANSIBLE
-------
-Ansible is an agentless, YAML-based automation tool .
-used to automate the configuration, deployment, and
 management of network devices and servers.
-It connects to devices using SSH or APIs, so no agent needs to be installed.

Main Components:
-----------------
1. Control Node
   - Machine where Ansible is installed.
   - Executes playbooks and manages all automation.

2. Inventory
   - List of managed devices (routers, switches, firewalls, servers).
   - Tells Ansible which devices to connect to.

3. Playbook
   - YAML file containing automation tasks.
   - Defines what action to perform and on which devices.

4. Managed Nodes
   - Devices managed by Ansible.(Cisco, Juniper, Palo Alto ,Linux, Windows ,AWS EC2, Azure VMs)
   - No agent installation required.
   - Connected using SSH or APIs.

Workflow:
- Log in to the Control Node.
- Run the Playbook.
- Ansible reads the Inventory.
- Connects to all managed devices using SSH/APIs.
- Executes tasks in parallel.
- Returns Success/Failure report.

QUICK WORKFLOW (3 STEPS)
========================

Step 1: Log into Control Node
$ ssh user@ansible-server

Step 2: Run Playbook
$ ansible-playbook deploy.yml

Step 3: Changes Applied to ALL Devices Simultaneously
✓ Real-time feedback
✓ No manual errors
✓ 15 minutes instead of 3 weeks manually

Benefits:
- Agentless
- YAML Based
- Easy to Learn
- Multi-Vendor Support
- Scalable
- Reduces Manual Errors
- Ensures Configuration Consistency
- Integrates with ServiceNow

Real-World Example:
If a security patch must be applied to 500 routers, instead of logging into
each router manually, we write one playbook and Ansible applies the same
configuration to all devices within minutes.


