CORE COMPONENTS (4 Main)
========================

1. CONTROL NODE
   - Where Ansible runs
   - Must be Linux-based
   - Can be laptop, desktop, or server
   - Alternative: Container (execution environment)

2. MANAGED NODES
   - Devices to manage (routers, switches, servers, firewalls)
   - NO agent needed (agentless)
   - Connected via SSH or APIs

3. INVENTORY
   - File listing all managed devices
   - Format: YAML or INI
   - Type: Static (manual) or Dynamic (from CMDB/cloud)

4. PLAYBOOK
   - YAML file with tasks
   - Contains Plays (targets groups)
   - Contains Tasks (specific actions)
   - Defines what to automate

====================================================================================================================

ADVANCED COMPONENTS (5 More)
============================

5. MODULES
   - Small programs that perform tasks
   - Written in Python
   - Examples: Configure interfaces, manage files, set users
   - Execute commands on managed nodes

6. HANDLERS
   - Tasks that run only when changes occur
   - Example: Restart service if config changed
   - Reduces unnecessary actions

7. LOOPS
   - Perform repetitive tasks efficiently
   - Instead of: Write same task 10 times
   - Use: Loop over list of items

8. BLOCKS
   - Group tasks together
   - Handle errors more effectively
   - Manage complex workflows
   - Execute in sequence

9. ROLES
   - Organize playbooks into reusable components
   - Include: tasks, variables, files, templates
   - Modular and easy to maintain

====================================================================================================================

ADVANCED FEATURES (2 More)
==========================

10. COLLECTIONS
    - Bundle roles, modules, plugins together
    - Example: Collection for Cisco devices
    - Simplifies sharing and reusing automation

11. PLUGINS
    - Extend Ansible functionality
    - Callback plugins: Customize output
    - Lookup plugins: Fetch external data

