IF INTERVIEWER ASKS:

Q: "Explain Ansible architecture"
A: "Control Node (Linux machine) reads Inventory (device list) and Playbook (YAML tasks).
   Uses Modules (Python programs) to connect to Managed Nodes via SSH.
   Executes Tasks using Handlers (conditional), Loops (repetitive), Blocks (grouped),
   Roles (reusable), Collections (bundled), and Plugins (extended functionality)."

Q: "What's the role of modules?"
A: "Modules are Python programs that perform specific tasks on managed nodes.
   They simplify complex actions like configuring interfaces, managing files, or setting up users."

Q: "What are handlers?"
A: "Handlers are tasks that run only when a change occurs.
   Example: Restart service only if config was changed."

Q: "What are loops?"
A: "Loops perform repetitive tasks efficiently.
   Instead of writing same task 10 times, loop over list of 10 items."

Q: "What are roles?"
A: "Roles organize playbooks into reusable components (tasks, variables, files, templates).
   Makes playbooks modular and maintainable."

Q: "Collections?"
A: "Collections bundle roles, modules, and plugins together for specific use cases.
   Example: Cisco collection has all tools to automate Cisco devices."

