17.Register:-
-----------
- The register feature allows you to capture the output of a task
  and later we can use it in a playbook,which is extremely useful
  when interacting with network devices like Cisco routers and switches.

Code:-
====

---
- name: Run show commands on Cisco IOS devices   # Name/description of the play
  hosts: ciscoIOS                                 # Target host group defined in inventory
  gather_facts: false                             # Disable automatic fact gathering (speeds up execution)
  tasks:
    - name: Run show version command    # Name/description of the task
      cisco.ios.ios_command:                      # Cisco IOS module from the cisco.ios collection
        commands:                                 # List of commands to run on the device
          - show version                          # IOS CLI command to display version info
      register: cisco_ios_command                 # Store the task output in the variable 'cisco_ios_command'
    - name: Display cisco_ios_command output      # Name/description of the debug task
      ansible.builtin.debug:                      # Built-in Ansible module to print messages
        msg:                                      # The message to display
          - "{{ cisco_ios_command }}"             # Print the registered variable's content (the show version output)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Dot notation :-we can also access the output of any register variable
using dot notation.

-In this example the output of the command will stored in the
 "stdout"=>standard out key.
-if you if you don't want to print all the Output information
 and just focus on particular part of the output then we use
 dot notation:-
 - "{{ cisco_ios_command.stdout[0] }}"        # Prints full output as one big text block
 - "{{ cisco_ios_command.stdout_lines[0] }}"  # Prints output as a list, line by line
 - "{{ cisco_ios_command.rc }}"               # Return code: 0 = success, 1+ = failed
 - "{{ cisco_ios_command.failed }}"           # Boolean: false = success, true = failed

    where,  - show version          # → stdout[0]
            - show ip interface     # → stdout[1]
            - show running-config   # → stdout[2]

- if we use this module "{{ cisco_ios_command.stdout[0] }}"
  we will have the whole so version command output,but if
  we want to extract specific information like software version
  from the output..

- apply the  regex filter useing pipe symbol.
 Method 1  :
  {{ cisco_ios_command.stdout[0] | regex_findall('Version (\S+)') }} # extracts only version number from full output
  1. cisco_ios_command.stdout[0]  →  takes the raw output of show version command
  2. |                            →  pipe sends that output to the next filter
  3. regex_findall(...)           →  searches for a specific pattern in the output
  4. 'Version (\S+)'              →  pattern to find: word "Version" followed by version number
  5. (\S+)                        →  captures only the version number (non-whitespace characters)
  6. output: ["17.3.1"]           →  returns only the version number from full output

Method 2  :
    - "{{ cisco_ios_command.stdout[0] | regex_findall('Cisco IOS XE Software, Version (\\S+)')[0] }}"

    1. cisco_ios_command.stdout[0]              → raw output of show version command
    2. |                                         → pipe sends output to filter
    3. regex_findall('Cisco IOS XE Software, Version (\\S+)')     → searches this exact pattern in output

    4. (\\S+)                                   → captures only the version number
    5. [0]                                       → picks first match from regex result list
    6. output: "17.3.1"                          → prints only the version number as string

