Magic Variables in Ansible:-
Definition:
- Magic variables are built-in variables automatically
  created and managed by Ansible.
- They provide information about the playbook, inventory, hosts, tasks,
  and execution environment.
- Unlike user-defined variables, magic variables do not need to be declared
  manually.

---
# ==========================================================
# File Name : magic_variables.yml
# Location  : playbooks/magic_variables.yml
# Purpose   : Demonstrates commonly used Ansible Magic Variables
# ==========================================================
- name: Demo on Magic Variables in Ansible
  hosts: ciscoIOS                      # Target host group from inventory
  gather_facts: false                  # Skip fact collection
  tasks:
    # Display commonly used Ansible Magic Variables
    - name: Display Magic Variables
      ansible.builtin.debug:           # Print output on the console
        msg:
          - "{{ playbook_dir }}"       # Directory where the playbook is located
          - "{{ inventory_dir }}"      # Directory containing the inventory file
          - "{{ inventory_file }}"     # Full path of the inventory file
          - "{{ inventory_hostname }}" # Current host being executed
          - "{{ ansible_play_hosts }}" # List of all hosts in the current play
...
===============================
Common Magic Variables:
1. inventory_hostname
   - Returns the current host name from the inventory.
2. ansible_host
   - Returns the management IP or hostname of the target device.
3. groups
   - Displays all inventory groups and their member hosts.
4. group_names
   - Lists the groups to which the current host belongs.
5. hostvars
   - Accesses variables of any host in the inventory.
6. playbook_dir
   - Returns the directory where the current playbook is located.
7. inventory_dir
   - Returns the directory containing the inventory file.
8. ansible_play_hosts
   - Lists all hosts participating in the current play.
9. ansible_play_batch
   - Lists hosts being processed in the current batch.
10. ansible_check_mode
    - Returns True if the playbook is running in check (dry-run) mode.
======================
Example:
- name: Display Magic Variables
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: false
  tasks:
    - name: Show Host Information
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg:
          - "Host Name : {{ inventory_hostname }}"
          - "Management IP : {{ ansible_host }}"
          - "Playbook Path : {{ playbook_dir }}"
